            <h2 id="what-is-ai-agent-governance">What is AI agent governance?</h2>
            <p>
              AI agent governance is the combination of technical controls and organizational policies that decides what AI agents may do, checks that they stay within those limits, and keeps a record of what they actually did.
            </p>
            <p>
              It is different from AI ethics frameworks, model safety research and feature settings inside an application. As we use the term, governance is operational infrastructure: a layer between an agent and the systems it acts on, which applies policy as actions happen and writes a record of each decision.
            </p>
            <p>
              Model evaluation asks whether a system is safe. Governance asks a different question: can we show what this agent did, on whose authority, and that it stayed within its mandate? The two questions need different tools.
            </p>
            <p>
              A 2026 Harvard Business Review article by Rahul Telang, Muhammad Zia Hydari and Raja Iqbal describes four organizational frictions in scaling AI agents: giving each agent its own identity and scoped permissions, making sure agents work from reliable data, adding deterministic controls around probabilistic outputs, and keeping accountability through audit trails and clear ownership. Governance infrastructure addresses each of them.
            </p>

            <h2 id="why-existing-approaches-fail">Why existing approaches fall short</h2>
            <p>
              Most teams start with tools they already have. Each is useful, and none was built for this job on its own.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Controls offered by model providers.</strong> Providers such as OpenAI and Anthropic offer usage dashboards, rate limits, content policies and some logging. These help with billing and abuse detection. But a log kept by a provider, under its retention policy and visible in its console, covers only that provider, and most agent fleets use several.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Application-level checks.</strong> Prompt design, output filters and validation before an action is taken are worthwhile safety measures. They live in the same application as the agent, though, so a prompt injection, a model update or a configuration change can weaken them, and the evidence they produce sits in the same stack that is being examined.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Observability tools.</strong> Tracing tools show what an agent did, step by step, and are valuable for debugging and performance work. They are built to observe. As a rule they do not block an action before it executes.
            </p>
            <p>
              What these approaches share is that each covers one component. <a href="/blog/missing-layer-agent-architecture/">The missing piece is a layer outside the agent</a> that sees every action, whichever provider or framework is involved.
            </p>

            <h2 id="the-four-requirements">Four requirements: identity, scope, enforcement, ledger</h2>
            <p>
              In our view, effective governance needs four properties. Each is necessary, and none is enough on its own.
            </p>

            <div class="not-prose my-6 border border-line-0">
              {[
                {
                  n: "01",
                  label: "Identity",
                  body: "Each agent needs an identity that is assigned and verified outside the agent itself, so that every action can be attributed to a specific agent, acting for a specific owner. An identity the agent asserts about itself is easy to forge. Without a reliable identity, an investigation cannot tell which agent did what, or on whose behalf."
                },
                {
                  n: "02",
                  label: "Scope",
                  body: "Each agent needs defined limits: which data it may read, which actions it may take, which providers and tools it may call. Those limits work best as policy that is machine-readable, enforced outside the agent and changeable without redeploying it. Limits written into the agent's own code can be bypassed by the same failures they are meant to contain."
                },
                {
                  n: "03",
                  label: "Enforcement",
                  body: "Some actions should be stopped before they happen, not just reported afterwards. A layer that only records is an audit tool. Enforcement means an out-of-policy action, such as reading a restricted source or calling a disallowed tool, is held or blocked before it reaches the target system. For high-stakes actions, finding out the next day is too late."
                },
                {
                  n: "04",
                  label: "Ledger",
                  body: "Each action needs a record that is kept apart from the agent and that shows later changes. Chaining every entry to the one before it makes edits detectable. The record is most useful when it captures context as well as inputs and outputs: what the agent retrieved, which tools it called and which policy applied at the time."
                },
