              ))}
            </div>

            <p>
              These are architectural requirements rather than a feature list, and they depend on each other. Identity without enforcement tells you who acted but cannot stop them. Enforcement without a record leaves no way to show what was allowed or blocked. And a record kept inside the system it describes is harder to rely on when something goes wrong.
            </p>

            <h2 id="regulatory-context">The regulatory context</h2>
            <p>
              Several regulations now ask for parts of this, even though none of them uses the word governance in this sense.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>EU AI Act (Regulation 2024/1689).</strong> High-risk AI systems, including those used in employment, access to credit, certain insurance pricing, education, critical infrastructure and essential services, must log events automatically (Article 12) and support effective human oversight (Article 14). Deployers must keep those logs for at least six months (Article 26). For Annex III systems these obligations apply from 2 December 2027. <a href="/eu-ai-act-compliance/">Full compliance guide →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>DORA (Digital Operational Resilience Act).</strong> Since January 2025, DORA has required financial entities to run an ICT incident management process (Art. 17), to manage risk from third-party ICT providers such as model vendors (Art. 28), and to secure access and audit rights in those contracts (Art. 30). For AI agents, that favors a record of agent activity that does not depend on the model provider. <a href="/blog/dora-ai-agents/">DORA and AI agents explained →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>UK regulators.</strong> In March 2026 the Digital Regulation Cooperation Forum (CMA, FCA, ICO and Ofcom) published a foresight paper on agentic AI. It is explicitly not policy, but it states that existing law continues to apply to agents and notes that accountability can be split across model providers, system providers and deployers. <a href="/blog/drcf-agentic-ai-foresight/">What the DRCF paper says →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>NIST.</strong> In April 2026, NIST published a concept note for an AI Risk Management Framework profile on critical infrastructure. Its examples include agents with tested and verified guardrails and systems that give traceable, auditable recommendations. <a href="/blog/nist-critical-infrastructure-ai-convergence/">How NIST, the EU AI Act and NIS-2 line up →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              These frameworks were written separately and impose different duties. They do point in a similar direction: know what your agents did, be able to intervene, and keep a record you can rely on. Teams that build that capability before a deadline will do it on their own schedule.
            </p>

            <h2 id="architecture">What governance infrastructure has to do</h2>
            <p>
              We think three architectural properties matter most.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Independent of the agents it governs.</strong> The layer should not rely on agents to log themselves, enforce their own limits or vouch for their own identity. It sits between the agents and the systems they act on and handles every action itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Independent of the model provider.</strong> Many fleets use several providers at once, for example OpenAI for some tasks, Anthropic for others and local models where data cannot leave the network. A layer that covers one provider leaves the rest unmanaged, so it has to work across all of them without changes to the agents.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Able to act, not only observe.</strong> Observability records what happened. Governance also decides what is allowed to happen and can hold or block an action before it reaches its target. For high-stakes actions, that ability is what separates the two.
            </p>
            <p>
              A policy on paper does not stop an agent at three in the morning. What makes it hold is noticing, during operation, when an agent does something other than its job, and having a control outside the agent that can stop it. <a href="/rogue-ai-agents/">How agents go off course →</a> · <a href="/ai-agent-kill-switch/">A kill switch for AI agents →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <a href="/blog/missing-layer-agent-architecture/">Why governance belongs in the agent harness, not the agent core →</a>
            </p>
            <p>
              <a href="/guides/governance-checklist/">A checklist of documentation and technical controls to have in place before an audit →</a>
            </p>

            <h2 id="vendor-landscape">Navigating the vendor landscape</h2>
            <p>
              The market for tools that oversee AI agents is growing faster than its vocabulary. Products in three different categories describe themselves as "AI governance", but they solve different problems at different layers.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><span><strong>AI security tools</strong> (for example Zenity, Lakera and Prompt Security) inspect inputs, outputs and agent activity for attacks such as prompt injection and data exfiltration. Their job is catching adversarial behavior.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Observability tools</strong> (for example LangSmith, Langfuse and Arize) capture traces of agent execution for debugging, evaluation and performance analysis. Their job is helping teams understand what happened.</span></li>
              <li><span><strong>Governance infrastructure</strong> (for example Kyde) applies behavioral policy as actions happen, writes a tamper-evident record of each action routed through it and runs independently of the agents. Its job is control and evidence.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              The categories complement each other rather than compete. A security tool does not replace a record of what was authorized, and an observability tool does not hold an action for review. Knowing which problem each solves is the first step toward a complete setup.
            </p>
            <p>
              <a href="/blog/ai-governance-three-categories/">A fuller breakdown of the three categories →</a>
            </p>

